    {
      "v": "timur",
      "t": "Эй, дорогой, присаживайся.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Hey, my friend, hop in.' The reflexive ending on the verb for 'to take a seat' turns the action back onto the subject.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Тимур, ты чего здесь?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Timur, what are you doing here?' Chef Viktor uses the casual singular 'you' with 'Timur', as close colleagues and friends do.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "timur",
      "t": "Елена сказала отвезти тебя.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Elena told me to drive you.' Timur uses the informal singular 'you' here with 'you', as friends and close colleagues do.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "timur",
      "t": "Надеюсь, у тебя хорошая подружка.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I hope you have a nice friend.' Timur uses the casual singular 'you' with 'hope so', as close colleagues and friends do.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "maks",
      "t": "Справедливость — это воздаяние.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Justice is giving someone their due.' Russian omits the present-tense verb 'to be' here, linking 'justice' directly with 'retribution', combining 'justice' with 'retribution'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "maks",
      "t": "Так сказал Цицерон.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Cicero said so.' Max delivers a punchy one-word reaction built around the Russian word meaning 'said'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "maks",
      "t": "Но это фигня.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'But that's bullshit.' Max delivers a punchy one-word reaction built around the Russian word meaning 'nonsense'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "maks",
      "t": "Цицерон не жил сейчас.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Cicero didn't live now.' The verb for 'lived' is in the masculine past tense, marked by the '-l' ending.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "maks",
      "t": "Люди обманывают.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'People lie.' Max puts the word for 'people' together with 'cheat' in a crisp, natural spoken turn of phrase.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "maks",
      "t": "Кто-то несёт наказание за другого.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Someone gets punished for someone else.' Max chooses the imperfective verb meaning 'to carry' for the ongoing action, followed by 'punishment' and 'other'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "maks",
      "t": "Жизнь сложная штука.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Life is complicated.' Russian omits the present-tense verb 'to be' here, linking 'life' directly with 'complicated', combining 'life' with 'complicated' and 'thing'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "maks",
      "t": "Но в этом есть справедливость.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'But there is justice in that.' Max chooses the imperfective verb meaning 'there is' for the ongoing action, followed by 'justice'. Now let's listen to the whole conversation again from start to finish.",
